We investigate the two- or three-dimensional Navier-Stokes equations defined within a bounded region. This model takes into account forces that act locally within the domain and a force that represents external influence, which we denote as $F$. In our analysis, $u_0$ represents the initial velocity condition in this model. This model is subject to specific mathematical constraints, which can be expressed as $\mathrm{div}\,u_0=0$ in the initial velocity condition. This condition is then coupled with a boundary velocity condition that is no-slip. This scenario represents a fluid dynamics system that is confined within a bounded region. This system can be expressed as
\begin{equation*}
\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
\partial_t u-\nu\Delta u+(F,\nabla)\cdot u+{\mathrm{div}\,}F=u\cdot\nabla F
\quad\mbox{in}\ D\times(0,+\infty),\\
u\mid_{t=0}=u_0,\quad\mbox{with}\quad \mathrm{div}\,u_0=0,\quad\mbox{and}\quad
u\mid_{\partial D}=0.
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation*}
